 ## Baud rate
 
-When choosing the baud rate, take into account that:
-* The Raspberry Pi is not very fast.
-* You will probably not be running a real-time operating system.
-* There will be other processes competing for CPU time.
+The way bits are shifted is picked from the baud rate:
 
-As a result, you can expect communication errors when using fast baud rates. So I would not try to go any faster than 4800 bps.
+* Below `burst_min_baudrate`: one high-resolution timer tick per bit.
+* At `burst_min_baudrate` and above: burst mode, a whole frame is bit-banged with the local
+  interrupts disabled (260 us at 38400 bps, 87 us at 115200 bps). Set `burst_min_baudrate=0`
+  to turn it off.
+
+Up to 19200 bps is reliable in either mode. Above that TX is reliable in burst mode, RX depends
+on the interrupt latency of the board, and full duplex is not reliable.
+
+Framing and parity errors are reported to the TTY layer.
+
+
+## Polling mode
+
+Setting `poll_cpu` hands both RX and TX to a thread pinned to that CPU, which samples the line
+`oversample` times per bit. The interrupt latency no longer matters and full duplex works, but
+that CPU is busy 100% of the time while the port is open.
+
+```
+sudo sysctl -w kernel.sched_rt_runtime_us=-1
+sudo insmod soft_uart.ko poll_cpu=3
+```
+
+The `sysctl` is mandatory, otherwise the real-time thread is throttled for 50 ms every second.
+Moving the other interrupts off that CPU is recommended:
+
+```
+for f in /proc/irq/*/smp_affinity_list; do echo 0-2 > $f 2>/dev/null; done
+```
+
+The cost of one sample and the resulting number of samples per bit are logged in `dmesg`;
+`oversample` is lowered automatically if the board cannot keep up. On a slow board,
+`poll_headroom=1 oversample=6` is a reasonable starting point at 115200 bps.
